<h2>The allegation and the first three attempts</h2>
<p>The prosecution case is grave: the petitioner is alleged to have stabbed the informant&rsquo;s father, who died of the injury, in an occurrence of 25 November 2022. Arrested and in custody from 5 December 2022, the petitioner sought bail three times and was refused three times — the ordinary fate of a named accused in a murder case while the prosecution&rsquo;s evidence is awaited.</p>
<p>The third round, however, produced something beyond a rejection. In January 2026 the High Court directed the trial court to conclude the trial within two months, and reserved liberty to the petitioner to renew his prayer for bail if the trial was not concluded within that time. That clause set the stage for everything that followed.</p>

<h2>What the fourth application showed</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The deadline lapsed.</strong> Two months were fixed in January 2026; by November, the trial remained unfinished, with only four of five witnesses examined.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The informant was not an eyewitness.</strong> PW-4&rsquo;s own deposition placed him at Bhagwanpur Hat bazaar, learning of the occurrence over the telephone — the case&rsquo;s ocular core was thinner than the FIR suggested.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Custody had run to four years.</strong> Detention since December 2022, against a petitioner with no criminal antecedents, kept lengthening while the trial did not.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bail followed.</strong> The State opposed strongly, but the court, on the facts and circumstances, admitted the petitioner to bail on a Rs 10,000 bond with two sureties.</div>
</div>

<h2>&ldquo;Liberty to renew&rdquo; — the clause that does the work</h2>
<p>Orders rejecting bail while directing expedition of trial are common. What separates a consolation prize from a real lever is the express reservation of liberty to renew on non-compliance. Such a clause does three things. It fixes a measurable yardstick — a date — against which delay is judged, replacing impressionistic complaints about slow trials. It pre-authorises the successive application, neutralising the objection that nothing has changed since the last rejection: the change is the lapse of the court&rsquo;s own deadline. And it shifts the explanatory burden in practice — once the court&rsquo;s direction has gone unmet, it is the prosecution that must show why custody should continue, not the accused who must show why it should not.</p>
<p>For the defence, this means a rejection hearing is never only about the rejection. Pressing for a time-bound direction with renewal liberty, and then documenting each date of the trial that follows, builds the next application while the current one is being lost.</p>

<h2>Where the evidence stood matters too</h2>
<p>The order is equally a reminder that successive applications are decided on the record as it has developed. By the fourth attempt, the informant had entered the witness box and revealed himself to be a hearsay witness to the occurrence itself. Bail courts do not weigh evidence as a trial court would, but the examined testimony belongs to the &ldquo;materials on record&rdquo; that legitimately inform discretion. A case that looked unanswerable on the FIR can look very different after the key witnesses have deposed — and the accused&rsquo;s continued detention must answer to the case as it stands, not as it was first framed.</p>
<p>None of this dilutes the seriousness of the charge. A Section 302 IPC allegation — Section 103 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita in cases registered after 1 July 2024 — remains the gravest in the ordinary criminal calendar, and three rejections show the court did not treat it lightly. What the fourth order recognises is that gravity authorises careful custody, not indefinite custody: when the State cannot bring its evidence home within even a judicially fixed window, the presumption of innocence reclaims its ground.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Diarise the compliance date in every rejection order that carries a time-bound trial direction. On lapse, move promptly: annex the earlier order, a certified copy of the order sheet showing the dates and the witnesses examined, and the custody certificate. The application should read as an audit of the court&rsquo;s own direction — because that, in substance, is what it is. Delay in invoking the liberty clause dilutes its force; the accused who sleeps on it invites the inference that custody is not pressing.</p>
</div>
